<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope John XIX</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1032">1032</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Romanus of Tusculum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pope John XIX, born Romanus, was pope from 1024 to 1032, a member of the family of the counts of Tusculum. A layman, he succeeded his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-benedict-viii">Benedict VIII</a> and received all holy orders in a single day. He crowned Conrad II emperor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1027.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-john-xix.jpg" alt="Pope John XIX" width="320" height="292" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bari_Exultet_roll_1_-_emperors_and_pope_(pope1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Romanus was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, the third son of Count Gregory I of Tusculum and his wife, Mary. While his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-benedict-viii">Benedict VIII</a> was pope, Romanus held temporal power in the city as consul and senator, so that the family controlled both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> and the government of Rome.</p><p>When Benedict died, Romanus, who was still a layman, was elected to succeed him. He was ordained in all the orders in succession and consecrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> so that he could take the papal chair, and he took the name John. He was closely related to five other popes of the 10th and 11th centuries, as well as to some of the most powerful rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> at the time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>John XIX supported Emperor Conrad II after the death of Emperor Henry II in 1024. Conrad and his wife, Gisela of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a>, were crowned with great pomp at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-peters-basilica">St. Peter's Basilica</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a> 1027. Two kings, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-iii-of-burgundy">Rudolph III of Burgundy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cnut-the-great">Cnut the Great</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, took part in the journey to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Cnut went to repent for his sins, to pray, and to improve conditions for pilgrims and merchants on the road to Rome, and negotiations over the toll gates Rudolph controlled were successful. In 1025 John sent a crown to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and blessed the coronation of the Polish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-i-the-brave">Bolesław I the Brave</a>.</p><p>His relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> worsened. He rejected a proposal by Patriarch Eustathius of Constantinople to recognise that patriarchate's sphere of interest in the east, and he is also reported to have agreed, for a large bribe, to recognise the patriarch's claim to the title of ecumenical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>; general indignation in the Church forced him to withdraw. He also invited the musician Guido of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arezzo">Arezzo</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to explain his musical notation and encouraged him to instruct the Roman clergy in music.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>John placed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cluny-abbey">Cluny Abbey</a> under his protection and renewed its privileges despite the protests of Goslin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Macon. He offered Odilo of Cluny the archbishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyons</a>, which Odilo refused, and the pope then chided him for disobedience. John died in October 1032. He was said to have been killed by a mob of angry peasants, but there is no direct evidence for this. His nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-benedict-ix">Benedict IX</a> succeeded him while still young; some sources give his age as 12, though he was more likely about 18 or 20.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pope John XIX” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Tusculani controlled papal elections for decades: Gregory's sons became Popes Benedict VIII (1012–1024) and <b>John XIX</b> (1024–1032), and his grandson Benedict IX was pope three times before being removed.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-tusculum">County of Tusculum</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1028, with the approval of <b>Pope John XIX</b> and Emperor Conrad II, it moved to Naumburg, a new seat founded by the Ekkehardine margraves of Meissen.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-naumburg">Prince-Bishopric of Naumburg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=1022" aria-label="Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pope John XIX"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=1022">What was here in 1022? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
